# Early Learning & Kindergarten Incursions

_Early Learning & Kindergarten Incursions from babies to pre-prep._

{% button label="Quick & Easy Quote" href="/quick-quote/" /%}
{% /hero %}

{% columns %}
{% column %}
### Early Learning and Kindergarten Incursions

Rhythm Culture's Early Learning and Kindergarten incursions are a fantastic way to bring music, movement, culture and learning into your early childhood curriculum. Our [Drum Sing Dance programs](/drum-sing-dance-program/) are packed with interactive, educational and multicultural experiences that encourage children to explore, participate and have fun.
{% /column %}
{% column %}
### Hands-On Music and Rhythm

With a wonderful variety of African drums and percussion instruments, every child has the opportunity to get involved and make music. Children explore their own sounds, learn fun and engaging musical patterns, and discover the elements of music, including rhythm, tempo, dynamics and sound.
{% /column %}
{% /columns %}

{% columns %}
{% column %}
### A Journey Through African Music and Culture

Our Drum Sing Dance incursions take children on an exciting journey through African music and culture, incorporating engaging stories, singing, dancing and activities that explore the body's natural rhythm. Through these shared experiences, children discover the joy of making music together, hearing their own sounds and enjoying the rhythmic beating of drums alongside their friends.
{% /column %}
{% column %}
### An Exciting and Interactive Learning Experience

With five different incursions to choose from, our Drum Sing Dance program offers a wonderful opportunity for children to experience music, movement and cultural learning in a fun, hands-on and engaging way.
{% /column %}
{% /columns %}

## Supporting the EYLF Through Music, Movement & Play

{% callout %}
Give your children the opportunity to belong, be involved, express themselves and discover the joy of rhythm with Rhythm Culture!
{% /callout %}

{% columns %}
{% column %}
### Supporting the Early Years Learning Framework (EYLF)

Rhythm Culture’s African Drum Sing Dance incursions offer a rich, engaging learning experience that aligns with the Early Years Learning Framework (EYLF) – Belonging, Being and Becoming. Through music, movement, singing and shared participation, children explore, discover and learn in a fun and supportive environment.
{% /column %}
{% column %}
### Interactive Workshops

Our interactive workshops create opportunities for children to develop a strong sense of identity and belonging as they participate, express themselves and experience the joy of creating music together. As children share rhythms, take turns, listen and respond to one another, they build relationships, develop social skills and strengthen their connection with others.
{% /column %}
{% column %}
### Let's Get Physical!

Drumming, singing and dancing also support children’s physical wellbeing, coordination, body awareness and confidence. Through playful exploration of sounds, beats and movement, children become curious and involved learners. They experiment, make discoveries, express ideas and develop their communication skills through music and movement.
{% /column %}
{% column %}
### Cultural

Importantly, our program introduces children to the richness of West African music and dance traditions. This creates opportunities to explore cultural diversity, develop respect for different traditions and broaden their understanding of the world around them.

Our Drum Sing Dance incursions bring together creativity, cultural exploration, physical activity and social connection. They provide a meaningful opportunity for educators to extend children’s learning through an experience that is joyful, inclusive and engaging.
{% /column %}
{% /columns %}

{% section tone="grey" %}
## Learning Through Music, Movement and Play

Our Drum Sing Dance programs offer young children a fun, playful and engaging way to explore music, movement, culture and connection. Through hands-on experiences, children are encouraged to participate, experiment, express themselves and learn alongside their peers.

Every activity creates opportunities for learning and development, supporting important skills across multiple areas of the early childhood curriculum.

{% columns %}
{% column %}
### Musical Development – Rhythm, Sound and Expression

Children explore the world of music through African drumming, singing, call-and-response songs and a variety of percussion instruments. They experiment with different sounds, discover rhythmic patterns, develop a sense of beat and learn to listen and respond to music. These experiences encourage musical awareness, creativity and self-expression.
{% /column %}
{% column %}
### Physical Development – Movement, Coordination and Body Awareness

Through African dance, movement games and rhythmic activities, children develop coordination, balance, gross motor skills and spatial awareness. They explore different ways of moving their bodies, practise following movements and discover the joy of expressing themselves physically.
{% /column %}
{% column %}
### Interactive Learning – Hands-on Participation

Every child has an instrument to play, ensuring everyone has the opportunity to be actively involved. Children experiment with sounds, practise new rhythms, take turns and respond to musical cues. The hands-on nature of the program encourages curiosity, confidence, participation and learning through experience.
{% /column %}
{% column %}
### Cognitive Development – Listening, Language and Early Learning

Music and rhythm provide wonderful opportunities to explore early learning concepts. Children practise counting, recognising patterns, remembering sequences, following instructions and responding to changes in sound and tempo. Singing and storytelling also introduce new words, language patterns and listening skills.
{% /column %}
{% /columns %}

{% columns %}
{% column %}
### Social and Emotional Development – Connection and Cooperation

Making music together creates natural opportunities for children to develop social skills. Through group drumming, singing and movement, children practise sharing, taking turns, listening to others and working together. They experience being part of a group, contributing to a shared activity and celebrating each other's participation.
{% /column %}
{% column %}
### Cultural Learning – Exploring African Traditions

Children are introduced to elements of traditional West African music and culture through drumming, dance, songs, stories and playful activities. They discover how music and movement are part of cultural traditions and community celebrations, encouraging curiosity, appreciation and respect for cultural diversity.
{% /column %}
{% column %}
### Creative Expression – Imagination and Exploration

There is no single right way to participate in music and movement. Children are encouraged to experiment, make their own sounds, explore different movements and express their ideas through play. These open-ended experiences nurture imagination, curiosity, creative thinking and confidence to try something new.
{% /column %}
{% column %}
### Learning Through Play, the Way Young Children Learn Best

Our programs bring together music, movement, culture and play in an inclusive group experience where children can participate at their own pace. Whether they are confidently leading a rhythm, dancing along, exploring an instrument or simply listening and observing, every child has an opportunity to engage and experience the joy of making music together.
{% /column %}
{% /columns %}
{% /section %}

{% columns align="center" %}
{% column %}
### What Happens During Our Drum Sing Dance Incursions?

Get ready for a lively, hands-on musical adventure! Our Drum Sing Dance incursions take children on an exciting journey through the sounds, rhythms, songs and movements of West African music and culture.

Led by an experienced facilitator, each session is filled with drumming, singing, dancing, storytelling, movement and plenty of laughter. Children are encouraged to get involved, explore new sounds, express themselves and experience the excitement of making music together.
{% /column %}
{% column %}
{% embed src="https://www.youtube.com/embed/zcPZmbtX0L8" title="Drum Sing Dance incursion video" /%}
{% /column %}
{% /columns %}

{% columns %}
{% column %}
### African Drumming – Drumming, Drumming and More Drumming!

Children are introduced to the African djembe and a collection of unique African percussion instruments. With an instrument for every child, everyone gets the opportunity to explore different sounds, experiment with rhythms and discover the fun of playing together. Children learn simple rhythmic patterns, practise keeping a beat and experience how individual rhythms come together to create a musical ensemble.
{% /column %}
{% column %}
### Body Percussion – Making Music With Our Bodies

Who needs an instrument when you have your own body? Children explore clapping, tapping, stamping, patting and other playful ways to create rhythm. These activities encourage listening, coordination, body awareness and an understanding of rhythm and sound.
{% /column %}
{% column %}
### Singing and Call-and-Response – Making Music Together

Through lively African songs, chants and call-and-response activities, children learn to listen, repeat musical phrases and respond to different sounds and cues. These interactive experiences encourage language development, memory, listening skills and group participation.
{% /column %}
{% /columns %}

{% columns %}
{% column %}
### African Dance and Movement – Let's Get Moving!

Children are introduced to fun, accessible African-inspired dance movements and rhythms. Through movement games and playful dance activities, they explore different ways of moving, develop coordination and balance, and experience the joy of expressing themselves through music and dance.
{% /column %}
{% column %}
### Stories, Songs and Cultural Discovery – A Journey Into African Music

Children are taken on a journey through stories, songs, rhythms and movement, discovering some of the traditions and cultural significance of West African music. Along the way, they learn about the instruments, where they come from and the materials and traditional techniques used to make them. This encourages curiosity and an appreciation of different cultures and musical traditions.
{% /column %}
{% column %}
### A Shared Musical Experience – Connection, Creativity and Fun

Throughout the workshop, children are encouraged to participate, have a go, explore their creativity and enjoy making music with others. They experience the excitement of playing as a group, taking turns, following musical cues and contributing to a shared rhythm.
{% /column %}
{% /columns %}

{% columns align="center" %}
{% column %}
### Workshop Details

-   **Duration:** 45 minutes to 1 hour, depending on the age group and centre requirements.
    
-   **Instruments:** African djembes and a variety of unique African percussion instruments are supplied.
    
-   **Participation:** Every child has the opportunity to play an instrument and take part in the activities.
    
-   **Activities:** Drumming, body percussion, singing, call-and-response, African dance, storytelling and cultural exploration.
    
-   **Experience required:** None! Activities are adapted to suit children's ages, abilities and developmental stages.
    

Our incursions are designed to be inclusive, engaging and developmentally appropriate, allowing children to participate in their own way while sharing in the excitement of a group musical experience.
{% /column %}
